{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* Day 54: `[]`, and every spec imports `describe`, `it` and `expect` from
       'vitest' by name.

       This line was `["vitest/globals", "node"]` from Day 41 until the first
       time anything actually ran, which failed with

           TS2688: Cannot find type definition file for 'node'.

       - because @types/node was never installed. A types entry for a package
       that is not there is an error rather than a no-op, and nothing noticed
       for thirteen days because `tsconfig.spec.json` had no files to check.

       Dropping both is better than installing one: explicit imports mean a
       reader can see where `expect` comes from, and the specs type-check the
       same way the app does. */
    "types": [],
    "outDir": "./out-tsc/spec",
    "composite": true
  },
  // Day 54: widened from the two spec-only patterns the CLI generates, which
  // do not stand up to being used.
  //
  // `ng test` builds the specs together with the app, so it was happy. Running
  // the compiler over this project directly was not:
  //
  //     error TS6307: File 'src/app/title-strategy.ts' is not listed within
  //     the file list of project 'tsconfig.spec.json'. Projects must list all
  //     files or use an 'include' pattern.
  //
  // - because `composite: true` requires every file in the program to be
  // listed, and a spec that imports the thing it tests pulls in the whole app.
  // The app's own tsconfig excludes the specs rather than listing only the
  // sources, and this is the mirror image of that.
  //
  // Line comments rather than a block, because a glob containing a star and a
  // slash closes a block comment early - which is how the first attempt at
  // this comment broke the file, with ten TS1005s and no mention of comments.
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"]
}
